text:
maya: Quick design question — how should the SDK expose client-side A/B hooks for rollouts? We need headers for opt-in routing + trace continuity. Thinking x-redwood-variant, x-redwood-trace. Example behavior ideas follow.

lucas: We should keep header surface minimal: x-redwood-variant: <variant-id> or absent. Don't add multiple variant values. Use tracing id separately: x-redwood-trace: <trace-id>.

maya: Two cases to support: 1) server-side canary (server routes) 2) opt-in client-side bucketing (client picks variant and sets header). Need deterministic semantics for fallback + how console visualizes traffic split.

anya: For client bucketing we need deterministic hashing + explicit opt-in toggle so apps can respect user consent. Also we must log variant in telemetry to the console and enable immediate rollback.

sam: Suggestion: header value format like `x-redwood-variant: canary-v2` and a second header `x-redwood-variant-source: client|server`. Avoid stuffing flags into a single header.

lucas: +1 to separate source. Server should only honor client-supplied variant if either: rollout allows client-opt-in OR request includes a signature we can verify. Otherwise treat as hint and re-evaluate server-side.

maya: Example SDK snippet (JS):
```js
// deterministic client bucketing (5% canary)
function pickVariant(userId) {
  return (hash(userId) % 100) < 5 ? 'canary-v2' : 'control';
}
const variant = pickVariant(userId);
fetch('/v1/generate', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    'x-redwood-variant': variant,
    'x-redwood-variant-source': 'client',
    'x-redwood-trace': traceId
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ prompt })
});
```

anya: Console UX needs: a `client-opt-in` toggle per rollout, a visible `min_requests` guard, and a `success_rate_guard` (pause/rollback when above threshold breach). Also show current percentage coming from client vs server.

sam: Operational guards proposal: require at least 1000 requests in the last hour and >=98% success for a rolling 5m window before allowing expansion beyond the canary band. Also enforce a rate cap per customer to avoid spike-induced failures.

lucas: Telemetry: add `variant` tag to spans and logs (trace-level). Console should show per-variant latency, error-rate, token-cost. For Dedicated customers, surface per-variant capacity usage.

devops-bot: Deploy scheduled for canary-v2 at 1767643250 :rocket: (automated)

maya: Action items: 1) SDK: add optIn boolean + include x-redwood-variant-source header when optIn=true 2) Server: validate or ignore client headers based on rollout config + signature support 3) Console: expose client-opt-in toggle + guard controls + client vs server split metrics 4) Telemetry: ensure variant tag in traces and cost accounting. I'll draft RFC/PR.

sam: I'll prototype the guard gating pseudocode and share a short RFC, incl. backoff + automated pause rules.

lucas: One more constraint: try to avoid adding more headers than necessary. Two headers max: x-redwood-variant and x-redwood-variant-source. Keep SDK ergonomics simple.

anya: Also call out privacy: don't log raw user ids. Use consistent hashed ids for bucketing and telemetry. Document the hashing salt and rotation behavior.

maya: Great — I'll open the PR for SDK changes and link the RFC. :thumbsup:
